---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { REPO } from '../lib/site';
---
<Base title="Privacy">
  <section class="wrap section">
    <p class="eyebrow">Privacy</p>
    <h1>Privacy on Unite4AI</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      You can read the site and draft a registry submission without an account. The site is hosted
      on GitHub Pages; public posting happens separately on GitHub.
    </p>

    <div class="grid grid-2" style="margin-top:2.5rem">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>What stays in your browser</h3>
        <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem">
          <li>Search text is processed locally. Your browser requests public index files from Unite4AI as needed; it does not send the search text.</li>
          <li>Registry form entries and local drafts are not saved to site storage or put into a URL or network request. Copying writes the selected draft text to your clipboard. The handoff opens a blank issue; you decide what to paste and post.</li>
          <li>Your light/dark appearance choice stays in browser local storage. Clearing site data restores your system preference.</li>
          <li>Unite4AI does not create visitor accounts or use advertising trackers.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>What leaves your browser</h3>
        <ul style="margin:0;padding-left:1.2rem">
          <li>GitHub Pages can log ordinary requests, including your IP, under <a href="https://docs.github.com/site-policy/privacy-policies/github-general-privacy-statement">GitHub’s privacy statement</a>. The site also requests fonts from Google Fonts.</li>
          <li>The browser may attempt a performance request to this site containing a metric, its value, the page path, and a timestamp. It does not include draft text or search terms.</li>
          <li>Posting an issue or pull request requires GitHub sign-in and is public under your GitHub identity. Do not post credentials or private information.</li>
          <li>Where enabled, Giscus loads from <code>giscus.app</code> and uses GitHub Discussions for public comments. Curated comments are published in the repository.</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:2rem">
      You can inspect requests in your browser’s network panel or read <a href={REPO}>the source</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
